package outlayer:signing-keys@0.1.0;

/// Signing keys for OutLayer WASI components.
///
/// A component declares the keys it signs with in its `outlayer.manifest`
/// custom section, at most three:
///
///     "signing_keys": [
///       {"path": "records", "type": "ed25519"},
///       {"path": "votes", "type": "ed25519", "caller": "predecessor"},
///       {"path": "payouts", "type": "secp256k1", "vault": "vault.owner.near"}
///     ]
///
/// `type` is `"ed25519"` or `"secp256k1"`. The type is an input of the
/// derivation: one path under two types would be two keys, and a path is
/// declared once.
///
/// The keystore derives each key inside the TEE, for this run only, from how
/// the code is run and the account that called for the run — never from
/// anything the component says at runtime:
///
/// - `bind: "project"` (the default): issued only to a run through a project
///   whose running version is published as a WasmUrl. The key belongs to the
///   project (its on-chain uuid, so a project deleted and created again under
///   the same name has new keys) and the caller; every version of the project
///   signs with it, so it survives a code upgrade.
/// - `bind: "wasm"`: issued only to a direct run of a wasm URL, with no
///   project. The key belongs to this exact build and the caller; a new build
///   signs with a new key.
///
/// A run built from a GitHub repository gets no key, and one declared key
/// whose `bind` does not match how the code is run refuses the whole run
/// before it starts.
///
/// `caller` chooses which account the key belongs to:
///
/// - `"signer"` (the default): the transaction signer on chain
///   (`NEAR_USER_ACCOUNT_ID`), the payment-key owner over HTTPS. Any contract
///   the signer ever transacts with can start a run under the signer's key
///   with input of its own choosing — a `ft_transfer_call`, a DAO proposal,
///   a wallet contract's callback all execute as the signer — so a module
///   must never treat its input as the signer's intent.
/// - `"predecessor"`: the account that actually called the contract
///   (`NEAR_PREDECESSOR_ID`) — the DAO or wallet contract itself when one
///   relayed the call, the signer when none did, and the signer over HTTPS.
///   The key is that contract's, not the signer's; on a payment path through
///   `ft_transfer_call` it binds to the token contract. A run with no
///   predecessor is refused before it starts.
///
/// With `bind: "wasm"` the key belongs to the code, so the code must not
/// decide WHAT to sign from its secrets, environment or configuration —
/// whoever runs the binary controls those.
///
/// **Never sign caller-supplied bytes or digests verbatim.** An ed25519 key's
/// public key is a real NEAR implicit account (and a Solana address); a
/// secp256k1 key's is an EVM address. A signature over bytes the caller chose
/// is a signature over anything those bytes are — a transaction that empties
/// the account, an authorization, a message the account never meant — and a
/// secp256k1 key signs a 32-byte digest as it is, so a caller-chosen digest is
/// the hash of any EVM transaction, EIP-712 permit or `personal_sign` message
/// the caller likes. Sign only messages this module composes itself, from
/// fields it has parsed and checked, under a fixed prefix or structure of its
/// own, and compute the digest here from those bytes; an input that asks for a
/// signature over raw bytes or a digest is refused. The same holds for
/// `sign-nep413`: its tag keeps the bytes from being a transaction, but a
/// caller-chosen `message` and `recipient` is a login, as the key's account,
/// to whatever site the caller names.
///
/// `vault` (only with `bind: "project"`) derives the key from that vault's
/// master; the vault must belong to the project's owner.
///
/// The component never sees a key: it names one by `path` and the host signs.
/// A call names the key's declared vault exactly: `none` for a key declared
/// without one, `some(<vault>)` for a key declared with that vault. An
/// undeclared path, a vault that is not the declared one, a key of a type the
/// call does not serve, or a message the key's type does not take is an `err`
/// carrying the reason, never a trap.
interface api {
    /// A NEP-413 signature in the shape a NEAR wallet's `signMessage` answers.
    record nep413-signature {
        /// The key's NEAR implicit account: its 32-byte public key, lowercase
        /// hex (64 characters).
        account-id: string,
        /// `ed25519:` followed by the base58 of the 32-byte public key.
        public-key: string,
        /// The 64-byte ed25519 signature, standard base64 with padding.
        signature: string,
    }

    /// The public key of the declared key at `path`.
    ///
    /// ed25519: the 32-byte public key.
    ///
    /// secp256k1: 64 bytes, `x ‖ y` — the uncompressed SEC1 point without its
    /// `0x04` prefix, as NEAR's `secp256k1:` public keys carry it. The key's
    /// EVM address is the last 20 bytes of keccak256 of these 64 bytes.
    public-key: func(path: string, vault: option<string>) -> result<list<u8>, string>;

    /// Sign `message` with the declared key at `path`.
    ///
    /// ed25519: an RFC 8032 signature (64 bytes) over the raw message bytes —
    /// no prehash, no prefix. At most 65536 bytes of message; sign a digest to
    /// cover more.
    ///
    /// secp256k1: `message` must be exactly 32 bytes, a prehash, signed as it
    /// is — no further hashing; any other length is an `err`. ECDSA with an RFC
    /// 6979 nonce, so one key and one prehash give one signature. 65 bytes out,
    /// `r ‖ s ‖ v`: `r` and `s` big-endian, `s` in the low half of the group
    /// order, `v` the recovery id, 0 or 1 — NEAR's secp256k1 signature and the
    /// input `ecrecover` takes. EVM wants `v + 27`.
    sign: func(path: string, vault: option<string>, message: list<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, string>;

    /// Sign a NEP-413 message (NEAR `signMessage`) with the declared ed25519
    /// key at `path`.
    ///
    /// The host builds the signed bytes itself: `sha256(borsh(2^31 + 413) ‖
    /// borsh(payload))`, the payload `{message: string, nonce: [u8; 32],
    /// recipient: string, callback_url: option<string>}` in that order, and
    /// signs the 32-byte hash with ed25519. Anything that verifies a wallet's
    /// NEP-413 signature verifies this one against `public-key`.
    ///
    /// An `err`: a key that is not ed25519, a `nonce` that is not exactly 32
    /// bytes, a `message` over 65536 bytes, a `recipient` or `callback-url`
    /// over 2048 bytes.
    sign-nep413: func(path: string, vault: option<string>, message: string, recipient: string, nonce: list<u8>, callback-url: option<string>) -> result<nep413-signature, string>;
}

world signing-keys-host {
    import api;
}
